VERDICT: PASS -- `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` lets several supplicants
         authenticate independently on one port, and each one is authorised on its own.
      feature off (single-host), 2 supplicants .. 1 admitted, the other got silence
      feature on, the same 2 supplicants ........ 2 admitted, 2 of 2 Authenticated
      a 3rd MAC with a bad password ............. EAP-Failure, state Held
      3 good supplicants at once ................ authorizedSupplicantNum: 3
      authorisation is per MAC, not per port .... an unauthenticated MAC on the same
                                                  port got 0 of 4 pings, then 4 of 4
                                                  after it authenticated
      one identity on several MACs .............. allowed; ckmulti1 held two sessions
ALSO MEASURED, and reported because an operator should know:
      `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` silently changes `dot1x control-direction both`
      to `in`.  `no auth host-mode` restores single-host but does NOT restore `both`.
      The proof is in the section after the steps.
CAVEAT:  Each supplicant used EAP-MD5.  This run does not show several supplicants
         using a tunnelled method such as EAP-PEAP at the same time.  The highest
         number of supplicants reached here is 3, against a reported limit of 1024.
         The local RADIUS server's counters are shared by every case in this group, so
         only the DUT's own log and the frames on the wire are used as evidence.
