ALSO MEASURED -- the host mode silently weakens `dot1x control-direction`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This was not part of the case design.  It is reported because an operator who sets
  `both` for a security reason is never told it was changed.

  Before: port1.0.48 carried `dot1x control-direction both`, and this case never sent a
  `control-direction` command.  After `auth host-mode multi-supplicant`:

awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.48
interface port1.0.48
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction in              <-- NOBODY TYPED THIS LINE
 auth host-mode multi-supplicant

awplus# show dot1x interface port1.0.48
  CD: adminControlledDirections: in
                                                 <-- no warning, no "% " line, no log entry

  Removing the host mode does not undo it:

awplus(config-if)#no auth host-mode
awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction in              <-- STILL `in`
awplus# show dot1x interface port1.0.48
  CD: adminControlledDirections: in
  hostMode: single-host                  <-- the host mode DID go back

  Reading: the behaviour itself is defensible, because traffic leaving a shared port
  cannot be controlled for each MAC separately.  The problem is that the device says
  nothing.  The operator's own configuration line was changed by a different command,
  and removing that command leaves the port controlling traffic in one direction only.
  The only way to find out is to read the running-config again.
  `dot1x control-direction both` was sent again by hand at the end of this case, and
  read back to confirm it.
